module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalGeometry.DerivedCompletionRinged.CompletionIdempotent
import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute

/-!
# Derived completeness as a containment of ideal sheaves, and Tag 099F for a globally generated
ideal sheaf

Let `(𝒞, 𝒪)` be a ringed site and `K ∈ D(𝒪)`. The sections of `𝒪` at which `K` satisfies the
Milnor condition form a sheaf of ideals `𝒟(K) ⊆ 𝒪`, which is [Stacks, Tag 0998] and is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompleteIdealSheaf`. The predicate of [Stacks, Tag 0999]
tests that condition at every local section of `ℐ`, so

`K` is derived complete along `ℐ`  ⟺  `ℐ ≤ 𝒟(K)`.

This file records that equivalence and the monotonicity in `ℐ` which follows from it, and then
records the conclusion of [Stacks, Tag 099F] in the case of an ideal sheaf generated by finitely
many global sections, which is the case [Stacks, Tag 099B] settles. The tag itself, for an
arbitrary sheaf of ideals of finite type, is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete` of `FiniteTypeReflector.lean`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isDerivedComplete_iff_le_derivedCompleteIdealSheaf`: derived
  completeness along `ℐ` is the containment `ℐ ≤ 𝒟(K)`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isDerivedComplete_of_le`: derived completeness is *antitone* in
  the sheaf of ideals — a smaller ideal sheaf imposes fewer conditions.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_of_le_of_forall_le`: the
  reach of the library's criterion for the tag, as a sandwich of ideal sheaves.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_of_eq_idealOfGlobalSections`:
  the conclusion of [Stacks, Tag 099F] for a sheaf of ideals generated by finitely many global
  sections, in the form in which a consumer holding an abstract `ℐ` can use it.

## Implementation notes

The statement here does *not* assume the tag's finite-type hypothesis; it assumes instead that
`ℐ` is generated by finitely many *global* sections, which is the hypothesis of
[Stacks, Tag 099B]. It is a special case of
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete` of `FiniteTypeReflector.lean`,
which carries the `@[stacks 099F]` attribute. The finite-type hypothesis is not carried along —
as `isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_of_isFiniteType_of_forall_isNilpotent` of
`NilpotentIdeal.lean` carries it for the other proved case of the tag — because it would be
carried *unused*: it is a **consequence** of the global generation, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isFiniteType_idealOfGlobalSections` of
`FiniteTypeIdealSheaf.lean`. So the hypothesis here is strictly stronger than the tag's, and
the case settled is a case of the tag.

The implication does not need `Epi` for the map `𝒪^r ⟶ ℐ`. The identification of
epimorphisms of sheaves of modules with locally surjective maps is available over a site, not
only over a ringed *space*:
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.EpiMono.epi_iff_isLocallySurjective` of
`StacksFrontier/Algebra/Category/ModuleCat/Sheaf/EpiLocallySurjective.lean`, stated for an
arbitrary `R : Sheaf J RingCat`; and it is not what the implication needs. The implication goes
through the *sectionwise* finite-type condition `PresheafOfModules.IsFiniteType`, which the
sheafification description `mem_idealOfGlobalSections_iff` supplies over every object of the
site at once, and then through `SheafOfModules.isFiniteType_of_presheaf_isFiniteType`.

The two proved cases of the tag are incomparable: the nilpotent one of `NilpotentIdeal.lean`
makes every object of `D(𝒪)` derived complete and its left adjoint is the identity, while this
one has the Čech completion `K ↦ R𝑆heafHom_𝒪(Č(f), K)` of `Completion.lean` for its left
adjoint.

### What remains for Tag 099F, and why the monotonicity above does not supply it

**Status.** [Stacks, Tag 099E] is proved for an arbitrary sheaf of ideals, as
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_isGlobalExtendedCech` of `FiniteTypeExtendedCech.lean`, and
[Stacks, Tag 099F] follows from it as
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete` of `FiniteTypeReflector.lean`.
Neither [Stacks, Tag 099D] nor any gluing is used there: the presheaf of Čech complexes of *all*
local generators of `ℐ` — a filtered colimit, hence a presheaf with no choice made — is sheafified
on `𝒞`, and on each slice the leg into it from the Čech complex of one generating family is
locally bijective on homology. Where the notes below say that 099E is missing or needs gluing,
they record the state of the library before that construction.

The source states the proposition for an arbitrary finite-type sheaf of ideals, one which is
only *locally* generated by finitely many sections. The criterion the library proves 099F with
is `isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_of_isDerivedComplete_completion`, which, for global
sections `f_1, …, f_r`, asks of the ideal sheaf `ℐ` exactly two things:

* `hI`, that each `f_i|_V` be a section of `ℐ`, which by
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.idealOfGlobalSections_le` says `(f_1, …, f_r) ≤ ℐ`; and
* `hΦ`, that every Čech completion `K^∧` be derived complete along `ℐ`, which by
  `isDerivedComplete_iff_le_derivedCompleteIdealSheaf` says `ℐ ≤ 𝒟(K^∧)` for every `K`.

So that criterion applies precisely to the ideal sheaves `ℐ` with
`(f_1, …, f_r) ≤ ℐ ≤ ⨅_K 𝒟(K^∧)`. What the library proves about the right-hand side is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isDerivedComplete_completionFunctor`, i.e.
`(f_1, …, f_r) ≤ 𝒟(K^∧)` — the two bounds coincide, and the criterion yields the single ideal
sheaf `ℐ = (f_1, …, f_r)` and no other. Monotonicity therefore does *not* widen the proved case
of the tag by itself; widening it needs either a strictly larger lower bound for `𝒟(K^∧)`,
which is a statement about the vanishing ideal of the Čech completion and so needs the
implications of [Stacks, Tag 0997] recorded as missing in `VanishingCriterion.lean`, or a Čech
candidate for an ideal sheaf that is only locally generated, which is
[Stacks, Tags 099D and 099E].

*The local reflectors do exist, and gluing them is still not an alternative route.* For a
finite-type `ℐ` and an object `U` carrying a finite generating family, [Stacks, Tag 099B] gives
a reflector on the localized ringed site `(𝒞/U, 𝒪_U)`, because a section over `U` is a global
section there (`Over.mk (𝟙 U)` is terminal); and the ideal sheaf it is a reflector *for* is
`ℐ|_U` itself, which is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.overIdeal_eq_idealOfGlobalSections_of_generates` of
`FiniteTypeLocalReflector.lean`, where the local half of this tag is proved as
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_coversTop_isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_overIdeal`.

The ideal sheaf 099B supplies the local reflector for, `idealOfGlobalSections (X.over U) _`,
agrees with `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.overIdeal`: for a
globally generated `ℐ` the agreement is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.overIdeal_idealOfGlobalSections` of
`OverIdealGlobalSections.lean`, and for a finite-type `ℐ` and a generating family of sections
over `U` it is the statement just cited. It needed neither a sheafification argument nor the
epimorphism/local-surjectivity comparison, only the comparison of the covering sieves of `J`
with those of `J.over U`.

What does obstruct gluing is a fact about reflectors, and it is not the remaining gap, because
gluing reflectors is not the route. The reflector
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletion` of `ReflectorCompletion.lean` is
`CategoryTheory.Functor.leftAdjoint`, determined only up to isomorphism in `D(𝒪_U)`
(`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_iso_derivedCompletion_of_bijective_precomp` is the
sharpest form available, and it produces an isomorphism, not an equality); objects of a derived
category do not glue along a cover from isomorphisms on the pieces. The source's own
globalization is performed one level down, on *complexes*, by sheafifying a presheaf of
complexes — see the section "What remains for Tag 099E" of `CechGlobalSliceAway.lean`.

**What the step from such a complex to the tag costs is now in the library**, as
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_of_isGlobalExtendedCech` of
`GlobalCechReflector.lean`: given a map `a : K ⟶ 𝒪` with the property of [Stacks, Tag 099E] and
a finite-type `ℐ`, the inclusion `D_comp(𝒪, ℐ) ⥤ D(𝒪)` has a left adjoint, namely
`E ↦ R𝑆heafHom_𝒪(K, E)`. Nothing is glued there: the reduction of `Reflection.lean` asks for
the *invertibility* of `Φ η_E` rather than the equality `Φ η_E = η_{Φ E}`, and so all three of
its hypotheses are local conditions — invertibility in `D(𝒪)` by the locality step of
[Stacks, Tag 08JJ], derived completeness along `ℐ` by the sheaf condition of
[Stacks, Tag 0998] — which are checked on the slices of the covering family of
`FiniteTypeLocalReflector.lean`, where `K|_U` is the Čech complex and [Stacks, Tag 099B]
applies. The complex itself is [Stacks, Tag 099E],
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_isGlobalExtendedCech` of `FiniteTypeExtendedCech.lean`, and
the tag is `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete` of
`FiniteTypeReflector.lean`.

